VALUES (
    'MIT-PORT-SCAN', 'Port Scanning Activity',
    'PORT_SCAN', 'MEDIUM',
    '', 'PYSHARK',
    'One source address contacted an unusually large number of destination ports on one or more hosts within a short interval. This pattern is characteristic of a port scan, which enumerates which network services a host exposes.',
    'Port scanning is normally a reconnaissance step: an attacker maps the exposed services of a host before selecting a target. It can also be legitimate - an authorised vulnerability scan or an asset inventory tool produces the same pattern - so the first task is to establish whether the source was authorised.',
    '1. Identify the source host and confirm whether the activity was authorised (a scheduled scan, an inventory agent, an IT technician).\n2. If it was not authorised, record the source address, the time window and the ports contacted as evidence.\n3. Review which of the contacted ports are actually open, using the Vulnerability Assessment results for the destination host.',
    '1. Restrict unnecessary services so that a scan finds less to work with; close or firewall ports that have no business requirement.\n2. Segment the network so that workstations cannot reach management ports on servers.\n3. Maintain an allow-list of authorised scanning sources in the Trusted Hosts configuration so that expected scans are easy to distinguish from unexpected ones.',
    '1. Re-run a vulnerability assessment against the destination host and confirm that only the intended ports are open.\n2. Watch the Network Monitoring screen for repeat activity from the same source.\n3. Confirm that any firewall change you made did not disrupt a required service.',
    '', 1, 1, UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
)
