    'TCP_SYN_FLOOD', 'HIGH',
    '', 'PYSHARK',
    'An unusually high rate of TCP SYN packets was observed from one source, with few or no corresponding SYN/ACK replies. This is the pattern of a SYN flood, in which half-open connections are used to exhaust a server''s connection table.',
    'A sustained SYN flood can make a service unavailable to legitimate users. The same pattern also appears during aggressive scanning or when a server is genuinely unreachable, so confirm the destination host''s state before concluding an attack is in progress.',
    '1. Check whether the destination service is still responding to legitimate clients.\n2. Record the source address, rate and time window.\n3. Review firewall and rate-limiting configuration in front of the affected service.\n4. Investigate the source/destination relationship before taking any blocking decision.',
    '1. Enable SYN cookies or equivalent connection-protection features on exposed servers.\n2. Apply connection rate limiting at the network boundary.\n3. Capacity-plan critical services and monitor connection table usage.',
    '1. Confirm the SYN rate returns to its normal baseline on the traffic chart.\n2. Confirm the destination service accepts new connections normally.\n3. Review whether the protective setting caused any legitimate connection failures.',
    '', 1, 1, UTC_TIMESTAMP(6), UTC_TIMESTAMP(6)
)
